Boris Johnson is a prime minister he  finds himself rather boxed in he's  predicated his entire Premiership on one  single aim to leave the EU or knocked  over the thirty-first Deal or No Deal  but how on earth is he going to do it  MPs have blocked his attempts to get a  general election at least try and get a  popular mandate from the people for his  brexit on Halloween  they've also passed a law requiring the  prime minister to seek an extension with  the EU if he fails to secure a deal to  avoid a No Deal exit on October the 31st  cornered but not surrendering he is  sticking to his own plan he will step up  negotiations with Brussels but he's also  clear of one thing he will not ask for  an extension set in him on a collision  course with Parliament and perhaps even  the courts by October the best chance he  has is to try and get a deal but if he  has a plan B to get us out without a  deal on October the 31st he's not  telling his cabinet or any of ours what  it is will he resign will he fight it in  the courts will he ignore the law and  run down the clock will MPs try to  collapse his government these are the  sorts of nuclear options that were  heading for come late October ask the  European Commission in public and  they'll tell you they're still working  to the assumption that the UK leaves at  the end of October but thought some in  private and they are increasingly  convinced that what will really happen  is Boris Johnson coming here to Brussels  in the middle of October for the summit  and asking for an extension now that  would have to be signed off by all 27 of  the other EU leaders and there has been  a suggestion that the Hungarians or the  poles might issue a veto as a favour to  mr. Johnson but in reality that's very  very unlikely in fact the only leader  who might conceive a vetoing extension  is Emmanuel macron of France who is  biblically fed up of brexit talk but I  don't think he'd really want to upset  the euro  applecart that much so the most likely  thing is an extension probably till the  end of January now talks are going on  between UK and European teams but  there's not much talk of concrete  progress instead so far we have no deal  no clarity and no real guarantee but  when Britain will leave the EU for the  moment it's all up in the air as this  threat of a Deal or No Deal continues to  hang over us the one thing we do know is  there's gonna be a lot more uncertainty  and uncertainty is something that  households businesses and indeed the  city don't much like it means weaker  growth the good news is the economy  isn't in recession and moment all  probably isn't it looks like we've got a  bounce-back in the last month and  employment looks relatively strong at  the moment bad news is it's nothing to  write home about this isn't very strong  growth and as we fixate on our domestic  crisis there's all sorts of things going  on around the world whether it's a trade  war whether it's China slowing down  Germany potentially being recession or  indeed a technology revolution that  could change the world that could be a  massive opportunity for Britain if only  we could start talking about something  that wasn't brexit
 

